<hr>
**Note**: Koka is a research language under active development.
The language is stable and the compiler implements the full specification.
The main things lacking at the moment are (async) libraries and package management.
<hr>

## Why Koka? { #why .heading-no-number }
~ begin banners
~ banner

#### Minimal but General
The core of Koka consists of a small set of well-studied language
features, like first-class functions, a polymorphic type- and effect
system, algebraic data types, and effect handlers. Each of these is
composable and avoids the addition of
"special" extensions by being as general as possible.

[Read about generality](concepts/general.html){.learn}
~
~ banner

#### Algebraic Effects & Handlers
Koka tracks the (side) _effects_ of every function in its type,
giving Koka rock-solid semantics with a direct correspondence to
well-studied mathematical foundations.
Effects are resolved using _effect handlers_, which let you define
advanced control abstractions -- like async/await, exceptions,
generators, or ambient state -- as ordinary user libraries.

[Read about effect types and handlers](concepts/effects-and-handlers.html){.learn}
~

~ banner

#### Predictable Performance with Perceus & FBIP
Instead of a traditional garbage collector, Koka uses _Perceus_ -- an advanced
compilation method for precise reference counting.
This deterministic memory management enables _reuse analysis_: Koka optimizes
functional-style programs to use in-place updates when possible.
This lets you write safe, functional algorithms that compile into
high-performance imperative machine code, a paradigm we call _Functional But In-Place_ (FBIP).

[Read about Perceus and FBIP](concepts/perceus-and-fbip.html){.learn}
~
~ banner

